\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Compact resolvent and thermal trace}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The Hamiltonian of \ref{fdip-00H2} has compact resolvent.
For #{s\ge1}, its eigenvalues #{E_0\le E_1\le\cdots}, counted
with multiplicity, tend to infinity. Put #{\omega=\sqrt{2a}}.
For every #{\beta>0},
##{\operatorname{Tr}\ue^{-\beta H}
 \le \ue^{\beta b}[2\sinh(\beta\omega /2)]^{-s}<\infty.}}

\proof{
\p{A bounded set in the form norm has bounded #{\|\cov f\|}
and #{\||q|f\|}. Let #{P_M} be the Fourier projection onto
#{|\xi|\le M}, and let #{\chi_R} be the indicator of
#{|q|\le R}. Spatial and Fourier tails give
##{\|f-\chi_RP_Mf\|
 \le R^{-1}\||q|f\|+M^{-1}\|\cov f\|.}
Indeed write the difference as #{(1-\chi_R)f+\chi_R(f-P_Mf)}.
For fixed #{R,M}, #{\chi_RP_M} is Hilbert–Schmidt: its kernel
is #{\chi_R(x)} times the inverse Fourier transform of a
finite-volume frequency indicator evaluated at #{x-y}.
Thus the form unit ball is uniformly approximated by relatively
compact sets and is precompact in #{L^2}. This is the cutoff
argument of \citet{Lemma 7.21, pp. 200-201}{teschl2014quantum}.
The form identity makes #{(H+b+1)^{-1}} a bounded map into
#{Q}. Composing with its compact embedding proves compactness
of the resolvent.}

\p{For the trace, compare with #{H_{\mathrm{osc}}=-\covsq /2+a|q|^2}.
Its form domain contains #{Q}, and
#{h[f]\ge h_{\mathrm{osc}}[f]-b\|f\|^2} there.
Choose orthonormal coordinates on the field space for this proof.
The resulting Hermite tensor basis gives oscillator eigenvalues
#{\omega(|n|+s/2)}, #{n\in\mathbb N^s}: dilate the
one-coordinate identity of \ref{fdip-007W} and tensor its basis.
The form-domain version of
\citet{Theorem 4.12, p. 141}{teschl2014quantum} gives
#{E_j(H)\ge E_j(H_{\mathrm{osc}})-b} for the ordered lists.
Summing their exponential bounds yields
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Tr}\ue^{-\beta H}
 &\le \ue^{\beta b}\sum_{n\in\mathbb N^s}
              \ue^{-\beta\omega(|n|+s/2)}\\
 &=\ue^{\beta b}
       \left(\frac{\ue^{-\beta\omega /2}}{1-\ue^{-\beta\omega}}\right)^s.
\end{aligned}}
This comparison uses ordered eigenvalues, not operator
monotonicity of the exponential.}
}

\p{The tensor basis and its concrete product identification are
formalized by \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.hermiteFunctionPiBasis}
and \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.coeFn_hermiteFunctionPiBasis}.
The one-coordinate differential premise is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.hermiteFunction_oscillator}.
Dilation, form comparison and the interacting trace estimate
are the deductions above.}

\p{For #{s=0}, there is one eigenvalue and
#{\operatorname{Tr}\ue^{-\beta H}=\ue^{-\beta U(0)}\le \ue^{\beta b}};
the factor with exponent #{-s} is one.}
